[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"HTTP Methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) in PHP"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"HTTP methods, also known as HTTP verbs, define the type of action a client wants to perform on a server. Understanding these methods is crucial for building robust and well-structured web applications using PHP. The most common methods are GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE, often referred to as CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"GET Method"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The GET method is used to retrieve data from a server. It's the most common method and is primarily used for reading data. GET requests append data to the URL as query parameters, making them visible in the browser's address bar. This makes GET requests suitable for non-sensitive data."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Example of a GET request in PHP
<?php
  $name = $_GET['name'];
  $age = $_GET['age'];

  echo "Name: " . htmlspecialchars($name) . "<br>";
  echo "Age: " . htmlspecialchars($age);
?>

<!-- Example HTML form for GET request -->
<form action="get_example.php" method="get">
  Name: <input type="text" name="name"><br>
  Age: <input type="text" name="age"><br>
  <input type="submit" value="Submit">
</form>"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data is appended to the URL."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Suitable for retrieving data (reading)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data is visible in the URL."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"POST Method"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The POST method is used to submit data to a server to create or update a resource. Data is sent in the request body, making it more secure than GET for sensitive information like passwords or large amounts of data. POST requests are commonly used for form submissions."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// Example of a POST request in PHP
<?php
  $name = $_POST['name'];
  $email = $_POST['email'];

  echo "Name: " . htmlspecialchars($name) . "<br>";
  echo "Email: " . htmlspecialchars($email);
?>

<!-- Example HTML form for POST request -->
<form action="post_example.php" method="post">
  Name: <input type="text" name="name"><br>
  Email: <input type="email" name="email"><br>
  <input type="submit" value="Submit">
</form>"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data is sent in the request body."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Suitable for creating or updating resources."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data is not visible in the URL."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"PUT Method"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The PUT method is used to replace an existing resource with the data provided in the request. It requires the client to send the entire updated resource. If the resource does not exist, the PUT method *may* create a new resource, but this behavior is not guaranteed and depends on the server's implementation. PUT is idempotent, meaning that making the same PUT request multiple times will have the same effect as making it once."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Example of a PUT request in PHP (requires more advanced setup, often using frameworks)
// This example demonstrates the concept, but a real implementation would involve handling raw input and routing.

<?php
  // In a real application, you'd parse the request body (e.g., JSON) to get the updated data.
  // For simplicity, let's assume we have the data in an array.
  $data = array('id' => 123, 'name' => 'Updated Name', 'email' => 'updated@example.com');

  // In a real application, you'd update the resource in a database or other storage.
  // This is a placeholder.
  echo "Resource updated (conceptually) with data: ". json_encode($data);
?>

// Note: Implementing PUT requests in PHP often involves using frameworks like Laravel or Symfony
// to handle routing and request parsing."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Used to replace an existing resource."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Requires sending the entire updated resource."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Idempotent (multiple identical requests have the same effect)."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"DELETE Method"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"The DELETE method is used to delete a specified resource. Like PUT, DELETE is idempotent. Deleting a resource multiple times has the same effect as deleting it once (the resource is gone)."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"// Example of a DELETE request in PHP (requires more advanced setup, often using frameworks)
// This example demonstrates the concept, but a real implementation would involve handling raw input and routing.

<?php
  // In a real application, you'd parse the request to identify the resource to delete.
  $resourceId = $_GET['id']; // Example: Get the ID from a query parameter

  // In a real application, you'd delete the resource from a database or other storage.
  // This is a placeholder.
  echo "Resource with ID " . htmlspecialchars($resourceId) . " deleted (conceptually).";
?>

// Note: Implementing DELETE requests in PHP often involves using frameworks like Laravel or Symfony
// to handle routing and request parsing."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Used to delete a resource."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Idempotent (multiple identical requests have the same effect)."},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"In summary, understanding and utilizing the correct HTTP method is crucial for building RESTful APIs and well-structured web applications. While basic GET and POST requests can be handled directly with PHP's `$_GET` and `$_POST` superglobals, more complex methods like PUT and DELETE often require the use of frameworks to handle routing and request parsing effectively."}]
